                },
                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "239406922",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198067829289",
                            "num_reviews": 11,
                            "playtime_forever": 71,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 71,
                            "playtime_at_review": 71,
                            "last_played": 1793848544
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "So, allow moi to preface my review with the fact that I have played Beat Saber a lot and I am fairly good at it, so comparing this game to that and the issues I mention, I don't believe it's purely a skill issue on my part.\n\nMy initial reaction to starting the first stage was excitement at getting to see Miku in front of moi (and dismay at having to hear levan polkka again 💀), soon followed by a bit of annoyance at how the game works. \n\nI quickly learned that as the notes come at you, they change colour (light blue/dark blue/red), so you can tell when they're close, BUT by the time they're red, they're too close already, that you'd miss hitting the notes, if you didn't otherwise notice em. Honestly, my biggest complaint about this game is the format of the notes. With them being flat and the the ring around them being hollow, I found it very hard to tell depth perception and messed up many times becuz of that. That's where the colours of the rings changing both helps and doesn't help so much. \n\nThe worst/hardest level for moi was Senbonzakura, just cuz the colourful background of cherry blossoms made it even harder to make out the notes. Not to mention the recurring issue of the notes coming from right behind Miku and through her body, which means half the time, I can't even see the note coming towards moi until it comes through her and then it's too close/too late =.-\n\nLike, I was straight up overwhelmed on that level. Levels 4~8(?) I think were hard, but the last two weren't. I just can't help but think that if the notes were presented in a different way, the gameplay would be easier.\n\nAnother thing is I honestly wish that when pausing, there would be the option to confirm quitting a level, becuz I somehow accidentally managed to both pause and select \"back to title\" halfway into a song 💀 ALSO, it would be nice if (similar to Beat Saber), when unpausing, there would be a few seconds delay, so you have time to catch up, as it unpauses. \n\nAs for the songs, I think the 10 available tracks are fairly decent, but with there only being 10 \"free\" songs with the game, I do believe you'd be better off buying this game on sale. \n\nOh! One more thing. While I was playing, I thought to moiself \"it'd be cool if they let you just watch the songs like a concert and not have to play..\" and then I noticed they do actually have a \"music video\" option 🙀 I love that. I found that part much more fun, cuz I could just dance along with Miku, instead of trying to hit the notes (not having time to even admire her dancing, becuz iam so focused on the notes). \n\nSo even aside from the gameplay, this is a cool concert experience and I'd argue it's better than paying to see Miku at an irl concert, only to see her on a screen, instead of as an actual hologram 💀\n\nThe only thing I found to be more fun/cooler than Beast Saber, is that the notes come at you from 8 speakers in a circle formation, so that was a bit fun, having more areas to hit than the standard 4 rows of notes coming at you in Beat Saber.\n\nOverall, I think this is a fun VR experience fwiw and as a fan of Miku, but.. gameplay-wise, I think it could be better. The gameplay prevents moi from wanting to keep playing more, but iam sure I would get tired of the same 10 songs overtime anyway and that's where Beat Saber outshines this once again, cuz at least you can download more song maps for that for free :'P",
